  "notes": [
    "import_resolution not measured: in-repo spec counts arrive with the L3 strategies (LB-F11)",
    "per-file times exclude the one-time grammar compile (an untimed warm-up extraction precedes timing)",
    "sym_precision (0.7852) fails L1 good (.90): universal-ctags 6.2.1's TypeScript parser loses class-body scope after a multi-line signature or a decorated/generic-heavy member, so it drops or mis-scopes real methods it should tag (e.g. nest's packages/core/injector/injector.ts: ctags tags only 'constructor' with scope 'Injector class' and reports every other method of that class, 50 by fathom's own tree-sitter count, as an unscoped top-level 'constant'/'function'). Reproduces even on this dialect's own fixture: internal/extractor/dialects/typescript/testdata/shapes.ts's `describe` method (a multi-line parameter list) is dropped by ctags entirely though every golden for it passes. Not a query-fixable gap — the loss is on the oracle side, not the extraction side.",
    "call_precision (0.5859-0.6417) is naive same-file call collection attributing a nested closure's calls to its nearest tracked enclosing definition, which a SCIP truth pair keyed to the (often untracked, e.g. an inline arrow-function callback) innermost lexical scope will not match; call_recall (0.78-0.91) is comparatively strong. This is the existing, documented naive-collection limitation (tsextract.go's own CollectCalls comment), not new to this reading.",
    "range_agreement not computed: universal-ctags 6.2.1's TypeScript parser never populates the JSON 'end' field for any tag on either subject, so the oracle side of the ratio has no denominator."
  ],
  "disposition": "deferred: tier B (universal-ctags 6.2.1) cannot prove L1 good on real TypeScript (sym_precision 0.7852 vs .90) because it mis-scopes/drops real class methods behind multi-line signatures and decorator/generic-heavy bodies; needs a tier C oracle (the grammar's own upstream tags.scm via the tree-sitter CLI) or an upstream ctags fix before L1-L4 can be proven here. LB-Q-typescript stays unchecked; see LB-Q-typescript-rest."
